rust/tutorial/custom-durable.content:
Advanced.
Most types should be derived or built from the containers.
Reach for a hand-written implementation when neither fits.
When you need one
Three cases.
A type with non-persisted fields — a struct carrying a cache or a handle that should not be stored.
The derive macro has no attribute for this yet, so you write an implementation that encodes only the fields you want.
An encoding you control precisely — you want your type’s bytes to be exactly so, rather than what the derive would produce.
A container — something with a non-trivial internal structure, which keeps an index of where its children start.
This is what the built-in containers do, and it is the hardest case.
If your type is a foreign one you cannot change, you probably want DurableBlob&lt;T&gt; instead, and to accept its costs.
The trait
pub trait Durable: Sized {
    const FIXED_SIZE: Option&lt;usize&gt;;

    fn encoded_len(&amp;self) -&gt; usize;
    fn encode(&amp;self, out: &amp;mut Encoder&lt;&#039;_&gt;);
    fn decode(input: &amp;mut Decoder&lt;&#039;_&gt;) -&gt; Result&lt;Self, Error&gt;;

    type Mut&lt;&#039;r, B: Backend + &#039;r&gt;: Mut where Self: &#039;r;
    fn mutate&lt;&#039;r, B: Backend + &#039;r&gt;(&amp;&#039;r mut self, region: Region&lt;&#039;r, B&gt;) -&gt; Self::Mut&lt;&#039;r, B&gt;;

    fn describe_local(builder: &amp;mut SchemaBuilder) -&gt; TypeDescriptor;
}
A value of your type is plain, in-memory data; the trait says how it is encoded into the file’s one sequence of bytes, and how a Mut mutates that encoding.
FIXED_SIZE is Some(n) if every value encodes to n bytes, and None if the size depends on the value.
A fixed size lets a containing struct find the fields after yours at constant offsets, lets a vector of your type find its elements by multiplication and keep no index, and lets a plain Vec of your type be durable.
encoded_len, encode and decode are the encoding: how many bytes this value takes, writing them into an Encoder, and reading them back from a Decoder, which fails on bytes that no value encodes to.
They never see where the value lies: the encoder appends to a buffer, and the decoder reads front to back.
mutate borrows the value together with its region — the substring of the sequence that holds its encoding — and returns the Mut your mutating methods live on.
A region offers write, splice, splice_value, move_bytes and paste, all at offsets relative to the value’s start and checked against its length, and child to make a region for a part of the value.
describe_local builds your type’s descriptor, which must match the bytes.
What to import
One crate. kladde re-exports everything a hand-written impl names, so you never add kladde-persist yourself:
[dependencies]
kladde = { git = &quot;github.com/kladde-dev/kladde-rs&quot; }
If you are writing a library on top of kladde-persist and have no reason to pull the facade in, depend on kladde-persist directly and point the macro at it instead — see the derive macro’s path resolution.
A complete example
A colour of three bytes, with a Mut that sets the whole colour or only its red channel.

use kladde::{
    Backend, Bounded, Decoder, Encoder, Error, Field, Mut, Durable, Region, SchemaBuilder,
    TypeDescriptor,
};

#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
pub struct Rgb {
    r: u8,
    g: u8,
    b: u8,
}

pub struct RgbMut&lt;&#039;r, B&gt; {
    inner: &amp;&#039;r mut Rgb,
    region: Region&lt;&#039;r, B&gt;,
}

impl&lt;B: Backend&gt; RgbMut&lt;&#039;_, B&gt; {
    /// Replaces the whole colour: three bytes, written in place.
    pub fn set(&amp;mut self, value: Rgb) -&gt; Result&lt;(), Error&gt; {
        self.region.write(0, &amp;[value.r, value.g, value.b])?;
        *self.inner = value;
        Ok(())
    }

    /// Replaces the red channel alone: one byte.
    pub fn set_red(&amp;mut self, r: u8) -&gt; Result&lt;(), Error&gt; {
        self.region.write(0, &amp;[r])?;
        self.inner.r = r;
        Ok(())
    }
}

impl&lt;B&gt; Mut for RgbMut&lt;&#039;_, B&gt; {
    type Durable = Rgb;
    fn as_durable(&amp;self) -&gt; &amp;Rgb {
        self.inner
    }
}

impl&lt;B&gt; std::ops::Deref for RgbMut&lt;&#039;_, B&gt; {
    type Target = Rgb;
    fn deref(&amp;self) -&gt; &amp;Rgb {
        self.inner
    }
}

impl Durable for Rgb {
    const FIXED_SIZE: Option&lt;usize&gt; = Some(3);

    fn encoded_len(&amp;self) -&gt; usize {
        3
    }

    fn encode(&amp;self, out: &amp;mut Encoder&lt;&#039;_&gt;) {
        out.bytes(&amp;[self.r, self.g, self.b]);
    }

    fn decode(input: &amp;mut Decoder&lt;&#039;_&gt;) -&gt; Result&lt;Self, Error&gt; {
        let [r, g, b] = input.array()?;
        Ok(Rgb { r, g, b })
    }

    type Mut&lt;&#039;r, B: Backend + &#039;r&gt; = RgbMut&lt;&#039;r, B&gt;;

    fn mutate&lt;&#039;r, B: Backend + &#039;r&gt;(&amp;&#039;r mut self, region: Region&lt;&#039;r, B&gt;) -&gt; RgbMut&lt;&#039;r, B&gt; {
        RgbMut { inner: self, region }
    }

    fn describe_local(builder: &amp;mut SchemaBuilder) -&gt; TypeDescriptor {
        TypeDescriptor::Struct {
            name: &quot;Rgb&quot;.into(),
            fields: [&quot;r&quot;, &quot;g&quot;, &quot;b&quot;]
                .into_iter()
                .map(|name| Field {
                    name: name.into(),
                    ty: &lt;u8 as Durable&gt;::describe(builder),
                })
                .collect(),
        }
    }
}

/// The fixed form, which `Fixed&lt;Rgb&gt;` holds: the same three bytes.
impl Bounded for Rgb {
    const FIXED_FORM_SIZE: usize = 3;

    fn encode_fixed(&amp;self, out: &amp;mut Encoder&lt;&#039;_&gt;) {
        self.encode(out);
    }

    fn decode_fixed(input: &amp;mut Decoder&lt;&#039;_&gt;) -&gt; Result&lt;Self, Error&gt; {
        Self::decode(input)
    }
}
Four things worth noting.
FIXED_SIZE and encoded_len say 3 because encode writes exactly three bytes.
Every offset computed by a containing struct depends on that number being right.
The Mut is a separate type, generated for you by the derive macro but written out here.
It holds the value and its region, and its mutating methods do both halves in order: record the bytes, then update the in-memory value, so that a failed append leaves the value as it was.
set_red writes one byte at offset 0 of the region, which is the red channel’s place in the encoding; the Mut knows nothing about where the colour lies in the file.
describe_local declares what the bytes are, not what the Rust type is.
Rgb writes three consecutive u8s, so it declares a Struct of three u8 fields — even though the implementation is hand-written.
Declare Opaque only when the encoding genuinely is not decomposable; see Your descriptor.
Bounded is optional: it gives the type a fixed form, so that Fixed&lt;Rgb&gt; exists.
A fixed-size type’s fixed form is usually its encoding byte for byte, as here; for a type with varints inside, it lays every integer out at its full width.
A value whose size changes
If your type’s encoding can change size, its whole-value set splices instead of writing: region.splice(0, region.len(), &amp;new.to_bytes()), which records a write where the sizes happen to agree.
kladde::replace(&amp;mut value, new, &amp;region) does exactly that and then updates the value.
For a large value that is replaced whole but changes little, kladde::splice_changed(&amp;region, at, old, new) records only the range between the common prefix and suffix of the old and the new bytes, as String’s set and DurableBlob do.
The region reports the change of size to the regions around it, which update their indexes, so nothing else needs doing.
A type with several parts, some of whose sizes change, hands each part a region of its own with region.child(at, len, node, index), where node is the type’s index of where its parts start, which the child’s region updates when the child changes size; the vector of kladde-types is the example to read.
A mutation that changes two places — a container’s element and its count, say — opens region.transaction(|r| ...), so that both reach the journal together.
Your descriptor
A hand-written implementation must also declare its type descriptor, and the rule is easy to get wrong:

Declare the descriptor that matches the bytes you actually encode, not the shape of your Rust type.

A struct with a non-persisted cache field declares the struct without that field.
Declare Opaque only when your encoding genuinely is not decomposable into fields, variants and sequences — an externally serialized payload, say.
“Hand-written” and “opaque” are different axes, and conflating them makes your type needlessly invisible to tooling.
The obligations you are taking on
A hand-written implementation is trusted, not checked.
You are promising:

FIXED_SIZE and encoded_len match what encode writes;
encode and decode are exact inverses, and the encoding is the canonical one for your descriptor;
every Mut method leaves its region holding the value’s encoding when its transaction ends;
your declared descriptor matches your actual bytes.

The compiler enforces none of these, but the third is easy to test: flush, and compare the file’s sequence with to_bytes() of the value in memory.

rust/tutorial/deriving.content:
#[derive(Durable)] turns your own struct or enum into a durable type, as long as every persisted field is itself Durable.
What to import
Two crates: kladde for the machinery, kladde-types for the containers.
Neither is on crates.io yet, so depend on the repository:
[dependencies]
kladde = { git = &quot;github.com/kladde-dev/kladde-rs&quot; }
kladde-types = { git = &quot;github.com/kladde-dev/kladde-rs&quot; }
You do not need kladde-derive or kladde-persist.
The macro is re-exported from kladde behind its derive feature, which is on by default, and generated code is rooted at ::kladde, which re-exports every item it names.
That is deliberate: if you had to add kladde-persist yourself, a version disagreeing with kladde’s would give you two distinct copies of the Durable trait and an error message that prints the expected and found types identically.
Here is a complete program:

use kladde::{Kladde, Durable};
use kladde_types::DurableVec;

#[derive(Durable)]
enum PhoneNumber {
    Mobile(String),
    Landline(String),
}

#[derive(Durable)]
struct Contact {
    email: String,
    phones: DurableVec&lt;PhoneNumber&gt;,
    starred: bool,
}

fn main() -&gt; kladde::Result&lt;()&gt; {
    let mut contact = Kladde::new(Contact {
        email: String::from(&quot;ada@example.com&quot;),
        phones: DurableVec::new(),
        starred: false,
    });

    let mut contact_mut = contact.get_mut();
    contact_mut.starred_mut().set(true)?;
    contact_mut
        .phones_mut()
        .push(PhoneNumber::Mobile(String::from(&quot;555-0100&quot;)))?;
    Ok(())
}
The Durable in that use is the derive macro.
It shares its name with the Durable trait, which is legal and deliberate — they live in different namespaces, exactly as serde’s Serialize does.
If you need to name the trait explicitly in the same file, import it under an alias.
Structs
The macro generates a Mut type — ContactMut — with a _mut() accessor per field, and a set that replaces the whole value:
let mut contact = /* ... a ContactMut ... */;
contact.starred_mut().set(true)?;
contact.phones_mut().push(number)?;
It also generates a ContactParts struct, and a parts() method on the Mut that returns the Muts of all fields at once, for when you need more than one at a time.
A tuple struct’s accessors are named by position — field_0_mut(), field_1_mut() — and its parts() returns a tuple struct.
A struct is its fields’ encodings, back to back, inline in whatever contains it.
A field’s Mut finds where the field starts: at a constant offset up to the first field whose size depends on its value — a varint, text, a container — and after that at the sum of the encoded sizes of the fields before it, which the in-memory values know.
Integers wider than a byte are varints, so a u32 takes one byte below 128 and up to five above.
That includes primitive fields.
bool, i32, char and friends all implement Durable, specifically so the macro can treat every field uniformly rather than special-casing leaves.
So do tuples of up to twelve Durable types, encoded like a tuple struct, Option&lt;T&gt;, Box&lt;T&gt;, which is persisted as the T it holds, String, and Vec&lt;T&gt; of fixed-size elements.
Fixed-size values
Fixed&lt;T&gt; keeps a value at the same size whatever it is: every integer at its full width, every char in four bytes, and every enum with its discriminant at a fixed width and its variant padded with zeros to the largest.
Use it for a field that changes often, such as a counter, which would otherwise change size whenever it crosses a power of 128, and for the elements of a large vector, which then finds element i by multiplication, keeps no index of where its elements start, and may be a plain Vec.
A derived type can be wrapped in Fixed only if it is marked #[kladde(bounded)], which requires every field’s type to have a fixed form too:

#[derive(Durable)]
#[kladde(bounded)]
struct Shape {
    x: u32,
    y: u32,
    kind: ShapeKind,       // also #[kladde(bounded)]
}

#[derive(Durable)]
struct Drawing {
    shapes: Vec&lt;Fixed&lt;Shape&gt;&gt;,   // eight bytes per shape, plus the kind&#039;s
    revision: Fixed&lt;u64&gt;,
}
Fixed&lt;T&gt;’s Mut replaces the value whole, with set(Fixed(...)): changing one field means building the whole value, and writing all of it, which for a fixed-size value is an overwrite in place.
Types holding text, a container, or themselves have no fixed form, and wrapping one in Fixed fails to compile with an error that says so.
A generic type marked #[kladde(bounded)] has a fixed form wherever its parameters do.
Newtypes
A single-field struct marked #[kladde(transparent)] is persisted exactly as its field — the same bytes and the same fingerprint — so wrapping a field in a newtype changes nothing in the file:

#[derive(Durable)]
#[kladde(transparent)]
struct Meters(u32);

fn walk(distance: &amp;mut Kladde&lt;Meters&gt;) -&gt; kladde::Result&lt;()&gt; {
    distance.get_mut().get_mut().set(42)
}
Its Mut’s get_mut() returns the field’s Mut.
Enums
An enum is its discriminant, as a varint, followed by the selected variant’s fields, each variant encoded like a struct in its own right.
So a value takes the size of its own variant, not of the largest.
In the schema, every variant holds exactly one value: a unit variant holds (), a variant with one unnamed field such as Mobile(String) holds that field’s type, and any other variant holds a struct of its fields, named after the variant, its positional fields named by position, &quot;0&quot;, &quot;1&quot;.
So moving a variant’s fields into a struct of their own changes neither the file nor the fingerprint: R { w: u32, h: u32 } and R(Rect), with struct Rect { w: u32, h: u32 }, are the same variant on file.
To mutate the current variant in place, match on the Mut’s parts().
It hands out the Muts of the current variant’s fields, in an enum named after yours, PhoneNumberParts, with the same variants:

use kladde::{Kladde, Durable};

#[derive(Durable)]
enum PhoneNumber {
    Mobile(String),
    Landline(String),
}

fn main() -&gt; kladde::Result&lt;()&gt; {
    let mut number = Kladde::new(PhoneNumber::Mobile(String::from(&quot;555-01&quot;)));

    let mut number_mut = number.get_mut();
    match number_mut.parts() {
        PhoneNumberParts::Mobile(mut digits) =&gt; digits.push_str(&quot;00&quot;)?,
        PhoneNumberParts::Landline(mut digits) =&gt; digits.set(&quot;555-0199&quot;)?,
    }

    // To switch to another variant, replace the whole value.
    number_mut.set(PhoneNumber::Landline(String::from(&quot;555-0123&quot;)))?;
    assert!(matches!(number.get(), PhoneNumber::Landline(digits) if digits == &quot;555-0123&quot;));
    Ok(())
}
Each field’s Mut writes inside its field, so the variant stays what it was.
set replaces the whole value, which is how the variant changes: it splices the new value’s encoding over the old one’s.
parts() borrows the enum’s Mut, so the compiler stops you from calling set while you still hold a field’s Mut.
The discriminant is Rust’s own: the one you write (Mobile = 1), or else one more than the previous variant’s, counting from 0; negative discriminants and signed or pointer-sized reprs are not supported.
The file holds it as a varint, so discriminants below 128 take one byte.
Inside a Fixed value it takes a fixed width: the smallest of 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes that holds the largest discriminant, unless an integer #[repr] such as #[repr(u16)] fixes it.
Explicit values on variants with fields need a #[repr] anyway, as Rust requires:

#[derive(Durable)]
#[repr(u32)]
enum PhoneNumber {
    Mobile(String) = 1,
    Landline(String) = 2,
}
Trait objects
A Box&lt;dyn Shape&gt; is not durable: a file can hold only values whose types it can name.
Hold an enum of the shapes instead, and let it implement the trait by forwarding to its variants: mark the trait #[kladde::dispatch], and the enum #[kladde(dispatch(Shape))].

use kladde::{Durable, Kladde};

#[kladde::dispatch]
trait Shape {
    fn area(&amp;self) -&gt; f32;
}

#[derive(Durable)]
struct Circle { r: f32 }
impl Shape for Circle {
    fn area(&amp;self) -&gt; f32 { 3.0 * self.r * self.r }
}

#[derive(Durable)]
struct Rect { w: f32, h: f32 }
impl Shape for Rect {
    fn area(&amp;self) -&gt; f32 { self.w * self.h }
}

#[derive(Durable)]
struct Frame { outer: Rect, hole: Rect, angle: f32, label: u32 }
impl Shape for Frame {
    fn area(&amp;self) -&gt; f32 { self.outer.area() - self.hole.area() }
}

#[derive(Durable)]
#[kladde(dispatch(Shape))]
#[repr(u32)]
enum AnyShape {
    Circle(Circle) = 1,
    Rect(Rect) = 2,
    Frame(Box&lt;Frame&gt;) = 3,  // boxed, so that a circle stays small in memory
}

fn main() -&gt; kladde::Result&lt;()&gt; {
    let mut shape = Kladde::new(AnyShape::from(Circle { r: 1.0 }));
    assert_eq!(shape.get().area(), 3.0);

    let frame = Frame {
        outer: Rect { w: 3.0, h: 3.0 },
        hole: Rect { w: 1.0, h: 1.0 },
        angle: 0.0,
        label: 7,
    };
    shape.get_mut().set(frame.into())?;   // `From&lt;Frame&gt;` boxes it
    if let AnyShapeParts::Frame(mut f) = shape.get_mut().parts() {
        f.hole_mut().w_mut().set(2.0)?;
    }
    assert_eq!(shape.get().area(), 7.0);
    Ok(())
}
The enum implements every method of the trait, each forwarding to the value its current variant holds, and converts From each variant’s value.
It is an ordinary durable enum otherwise: you mutate a variant through parts(), and switch variants with set.
Each variant holds one value, whose type implements the trait, or the enum fails to compile, at the variant.
The discriminants are optional, as on every enum, but explicit ones keep your files readable when you add or reorder implementations.
A variant may box its value, which keeps one large implementation from making every value of the enum as large in memory; it changes nothing in the file, so you can box or unbox a variant later.
The trait’s methods must take self, &amp;self or &amp;mut self, and mention Self nowhere else, and the trait and the enum must live in one crate; the derive macro says why.
Generics
Type parameters work, and each gets a Durable bound:
#[derive(Durable)]
struct Labelled&lt;T&gt; {
    label: String,
    value: T,
}
Lifetime and const parameters are not supported.
What the derive needs
Every persisted field must be Durable.
In practice that means:

primitives and String — fine as-is;
Vec&lt;T&gt; — fine where T is fixed-size; for any other T it fails to compile, and the message points you to DurableVec&lt;T&gt;;
kladde containers — fine as-is;
your own derived types — fine, and they nest arbitrarily;
HashMap&lt;K, V&gt;, PathBuf and other standard types — not Durable.
Use the kladde equivalents, such as DurableMap&lt;K, V&gt;.
This is deliberate: a field’s type says whether it is durable, and the compile error tells you so.

Non-persisted fields
Sometimes a struct has a field that should not be stored — a cache, a handle, something derived.
The derive macro has no attribute for this yet; the workaround is a hand-written implementation that encodes only the fields you want persisted.
Doing so does not make your type opaque to the format.
A hand-written implementation that encodes exactly the bytes of a struct with two fields has that struct’s descriptor.
The presence of a third, non-persisted field in your Rust source is invisible.
Encoding stability
A few things you should know before you ship a file format built on a derived type.
Field order is encoding.
Reordering fields in the source changes the order of their encodings, and therefore changes the type’s fingerprint, and Kladde::open refuses a file whose fingerprint differs from your type’s.
Once evolution lands, reordering will be non-breaking, because fields are identified by name.
Variant order is encoding only through implicit discriminants.
A variant without an explicit discriminant takes the next number, so inserting or reordering variants renumbers the ones after them, and changes the fingerprint.
With explicit discriminants, reordering changes nothing, since enum variants are canonicalized by discriminant value.
The discriminant’s width in a Fixed value is encoding too, unless you pin it.
Without a #[repr], adding a 257th variant, or giving one a discriminant of 256 or more, widens the discriminant of every Fixed value of the type, and changes the fingerprint.
An integer #[repr], such as #[repr(u16)], fixes the width up front.
Renaming a type is free.
A struct’s or enum’s own name is not fingerprinted.
Renaming a field or a variant is not free, since those are the identities evolution reconciles by.

rust/tutorial/durability.content:
What is guaranteed when, and what a crash actually costs you.
The two-stage model
A mutation does two things before it returns.

It appends records describing the byte-level change to the journal.
It updates the in-memory value.

Separately and later, the journal is folded: the records are applied to the file’s compact form, written to fresh pages, and a new journal is started.
Durability comes from the journal, not from the fold.
A mutation that has returned survives an application crash whether or not a fold has happened, because its record is in the file — the operating system writes it out even if your process dies.
A power cut is the case where the fold matters; see below.
What a crash costs
After an application crash: nothing that returned Ok.
If push returned Ok, the element is in the file, and Kladde::open finds it.
After a power cut: at most what came after the last flush.
Journal appends are not forced to disk one by one, so a power cut can take the ones the operating system had not written out yet.
Everything folded by a completed flush survives, and what survives of the rest is always a prefix: never a later mutation without the earlier ones.
A partial mutation is never visible.
Each mutation is one transaction, and so is each transaction you open, so recovery replays all of it or none of it: a push that splices in an element and rewrites the vector’s count is never half there.
A crash during a fold costs the fold, not the data.
The fold never overwrites live data in place; it writes to unused space and commits with one small atomic write.
A crash before that commit leaves the old state intact and the half-written new data inert, and the journal still holds everything, so the next open simply redoes the fold.
Flushing
You do not normally have to think about this.
Flushing is automatic: a mutation that would not fit in what is left of the journal’s budget folds the journal before it returns.
Application authors mutate state and never call anything.
An explicit flush() remains available for the cases where you want the fold to happen now — before a long idle period, say, since a flush is also what makes everything before it survive a power cut.
An interactive application calls flush_if_journal_exceeds(0.5) from its idle callback.
It flushes only if the journal holds at least that fraction of its budget, and returns whether it did, so it costs next to nothing when there is little to fold.
Flushing while the user pauses keeps the automatic flush, whose fsync blocks the mutation that triggers it, out of the middle of a gesture such as a drag.
Keep the fraction well above zero: a flush ends the journal, and with it every cut that is still waiting to be pasted, which is then encoded again when it is pasted.
if journal.flush_if_journal_exceeds(0.5)? {
    // folded while the user paused
}
close() flushes and then truncates the file after its last live page, which does not shrink it to its live pages: free pages below that one stay in the file.
They go only as later flushes consolidate, each moving a bounded amount of live content down; space freed by one flush becomes available from the flush after it on.
To reclaim more of it before closing, call flush() a few times first; a freshly created file, for instance, needs one or two:
journal.flush()?;
journal.flush()?;
journal.close()?;
What flushing is not for: making your data survive an application crash.
The journal already did that.
When the disk fails
A failed fsync means the operating system may have dropped writes it had already accepted, so kladde poisons the Kladde: every later call returns Error::Poisoned.
Drop it and open the file again; it then holds every mutation that returned Ok.
Transactions
Every mutation is atomic on its own.
When several belong together — money leaving one account must arrive in another — make them one transaction, and a crash keeps all of them or none:

use kladde::{Kladde, Durable};

#[derive(Durable)]
struct Accounts {
    checking: i64,
    savings: i64,
}

fn main() -&gt; kladde::Result&lt;()&gt; {
    let mut accounts = Kladde::new(Accounts { checking: 100, savings: 0 });

    let mut tx = accounts.transaction();
    let mut both = tx.get_mut();
    let AccountsParts { mut checking, mut savings } = both.parts();
    checking.set(70)?;
    savings.set(30)?;
    tx.commit()?;

    assert_eq!(accounts.get().savings, 30);
    Ok(())
}
Each mutation still changes the in-memory value when it is made, and commit appends them to the journal together.
Dropping a transaction commits it too, unless a panic is unwinding: then its mutations are discarded, since they may be half a change, and the Kladde is poisoned, since the in-memory value already holds them.
There is no way to cancel a transaction, for the same reason.
Transactions has the details.
Long runs of mutations
A single transaction is held in memory until it ends, because the fold only ever folds complete transactions.
So keep transactions short, and use a batch for a long run of mutations that are each valid on their own:

let mut batch = log.batch();
for reading in 0..100_000 {
    batch.get_mut().push(reading)?;
}
batch.end()?;
A batch appends its mutations to the journal in a few large pieces rather than one by one, which saves writes, and the automatic flush can fold between the pieces.
It is not a transaction: a crash can keep some pieces and lose the later ones, though never part of a mutation, nor of a transaction opened inside the batch with batch.transaction().

rust/tutorial/getting-started.content:
The mental model
A durable data structure lives in two places at once.
In memory, it is an ordinary Rust value: a DurableVec&lt;T&gt; really does hold a Vec&lt;T&gt;, and reading from it is a plain memory access.
Nothing about reading touches the file.
In the file, it is a compact binary representation that lags slightly behind, plus a journal of changes not yet folded into it.
A mutation appends to the journal and then updates the in-memory value, before it returns.
So: reads are as fast as the plain in-memory equivalent, writes cost one small append, and the bulk representation is brought up to date periodically rather than on every change.
The name comes from this shape.
A Kladde is a merchant’s rough day-book — transactions scribbled down in order as they happen — later transcribed into the clean main ledger.
Your first durable value

use kladde::{Kladde, Durable};
use kladde_types::DurableVec;

#[derive(Durable)]
struct Journal {
    owner: String,
    entries: DurableVec&lt;String&gt;,
}

fn main() -&gt; kladde::Result&lt;()&gt; {
    let path = std::env::temp_dir().join(&quot;getting-started.kladde&quot;);
    let mut journal = Kladde::create(
        &amp;path,
        Journal {
            owner: String::from(&quot;ada&quot;),
            entries: DurableVec::new(),
        },
    )?;

    journal
        .get_mut()
        .entries_mut()
        .push(String::from(&quot;first entry&quot;))?;
    println!(&quot;{} entries&quot;, journal.get().entries.len());
    journal.close()?;

    let journal = Kladde::&lt;Journal&gt;::open(&amp;path)?;
    assert_eq!(journal.get().entries.len(), 1);
    Ok(())
}
Four things are worth noticing.
Kladde&lt;T&gt; is the root.
It pairs your root value with the file that backs it.
Everything reachable from it is durable; a value you construct outside it is not, until you store it into something that is.
Reading goes through get(), which hands you &amp;T — the plain value.
From there you use ordinary methods: len(), iter(), indexing.
No Mut, no file access, no cost beyond the read itself.
Writing goes through get_mut(), which hands you a JournalMut.
Every field of a derived type gets a _mut() accessor on its Mut, which reborrows the same backend one level deeper.
So journal.get_mut().entries_mut() is a DurableVecMut, and push on it both records the change and mutates the vector.
It returns a Result, because recording is I/O.
open reads it all back.
close folds the journal into the file first, but it does not have to: a file closed by a crash opens just the same, with every mutation that returned Ok.
Kladde::new(value) does the same as create with a file that lives only in memory, which is handy for tests and for trying things out.
Why the Mut exists
In Python, journal.entries.append(x) could persist automatically, because Python lets a library intercept attribute access.
Rust does not.
There is no hook that fires when you assign to a field.
The Mut is the substitute.
It stands where a &amp;mut would — get_mut() hands it out as Vec::get_mut hands out a &amp;mut T.
It is an RAII token that proves you have exclusive, recording access, and it carries two things a plain &amp;mut could not: the backend to record into, and the region of the file’s content that this particular field occupies — where its encoding starts in the file’s one sequence of bytes, and how long it is.
This is why you cannot mutate a durable value through a plain &amp;mut.
There is no such method.
Persistence is not something you can forget, because the only mutating API is the one that records.
Nesting
Muts compose to arbitrary depth, and each level reborrows the same backend:

let mut journal_mut = journal.get_mut();
let mut entries = journal_mut.entries_mut();
entries.push(String::from(&quot;second&quot;))?;
entries.push(String::from(&quot;third&quot;))?;
Or in one chain, when you only need one mutation:

journal.get_mut().owner_mut().set(&quot;grace&quot;)?;
Each _mut() borrows the Mut it is called on, so a Mut hands out one field’s Mut at a time.
To mutate several fields together, parts() hands out a Mut for every field at once, in a struct named after yours:

let mut journal_mut = journal.get_mut();
let JournalParts { mut owner, mut entries } = journal_mut.parts();
owner.set(&quot;grace&quot;)?;
entries.push(String::from(&quot;handed over&quot;))?;
Hold a Mut only as long as you need it.
It borrows the root, so nothing else can touch the structure while it is alive.
